-- allium: 3
--
-- JEBOL: putting a gob tree on a real screen, and getting events back.
--
-- Scope: the three commands a windowing host must answer -- INIT-TOP-WINDOW,
-- GUI-METRIC and SHOW -- the port behind them, and the queue that carries
-- events from the screen back to the script. Together these are what
-- `view-funcs.reb` needs, and that file is REBOL's own: VIEW, UNVIEW,
-- DO-EVENTS and the handler list are borrowed and never rewritten here.
--
-- Excludes:
--   - The DRAW dialect. A gob whose content is a draw block carries thirty
--     commands declared in boot/draw.reb, and painting them is a separate
--     piece of work with a spec of its own. Here a gob paints its colour,
--     its text and its image, and a draw block paints nothing yet.
--   - VID. There is no VID in REBOL 3: LAYOUT is defined nowhere in the
--     mezzanine or the boot files, and `view-funcs.reb` calls it anyway.
--     Whatever JEBOL grows there is JEBOL's own and is written in REBOL.
--   - The five dialogs that ask the operator a question. Those are
--     WindowRequest in embed.allium and share this grant and nothing else.
--   - Native widgets. GOBT_WIDGET is a content kind and the win32 host
--     serves thirteen of them; no gob painted here is one.
--
-- Dependencies: GobStorage and GobContent in values.allium. The `windows`
-- member of HostService, and ServiceRefusal, in embed.allium.
--
-- The authority is the C, and for this area the C is the host rather than
-- the core: src/os/posix/host-window.c dispatches all three commands, and
-- src/os/win32/host-window.c serves the same twelve metrics a different way.
-- Where the two hosts disagree, they disagree about how much they implement
-- and never about what a command means.

use "./values.allium" as values

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What GUI-METRIC can be asked for.
--
-- Twelve, and both hosts serve exactly these twelve. `virtual-screen-size` is
-- in the word list boot/window.reb hands the host and is in neither host's
-- switch, so asking for it is refused everywhere; it is not a member here for
-- that reason.
--
-- Eleven answer a pair. `screens` answers a count and is the only one that
-- does not, which is why it cannot be folded into the others.
enum ScreenMetric {
    screen_size          -- the whole display, in pixels
    | screen_origin      -- where this display starts in the desktop
    | screen_dpi         -- dots per inch, across and down
    | screens            -- how many displays there are; a count, not a pair
    | work_origin        -- where the usable area starts, inside the furniture
    | work_size          -- how big the usable area is
    | title_size         -- the height of a title bar
    | border_size        -- the frame around a window that can be resized
    | border_fixed       -- the frame around a window that cannot
    | window_min_size    -- the smallest a window is allowed to be
    | log_size           -- the scaling factor from logical to physical pixels
    | phys_size          -- its reciprocal
}

-- Where a gob stands in relation to a window on the screen.
--
-- The C holds this as two independent bits rather than as a state: whether
-- the gob has a parent, and whether GOBF_WINDOW is set on it. SHOW reads the
-- pair and decides what to do, which is why its comment has to explain that
-- "a new window will be in Gob_Root/pane but will not have GOBF_WINDOW set"
-- and that "a closed window will have no PARENT and will not be in the
-- Gob_Root/pane but will have GOBF_WINDOW set". Naming the three combinations
-- that mean something is the same fact said once.
enum WindowStanding {
    asked_for      -- in the root's pane, with no window yet: open it
    | showing      -- in the root's pane, with a window: refresh it
    | dismissed    -- has a window and has left the root's pane: close it
}

-- What the screen tells a script about. The names are EventCatalogue's,
-- which are the C's, and the order there cannot change because reb-evtypes.h
-- is generated from it.
--
-- These eight are what a window can say in this increment. The catalogue has
-- forty-odd; the rest belong to ports, to the console and to devices.
enum ScreenEventKind {
    close          -- the operator asked for the window to go away
    | resize       -- the window changed size
    | offset       -- the window moved
    | key          -- a key that types a character went down
    | key_up       -- a key that types a character came up
    | control      -- a key that types nothing went down: an arrow, f1, escape
    | control_up   -- a key that types nothing came up
    | down         -- a pointer button went down
    | up           -- a pointer button came up
    | move         -- the pointer moved
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The screen a host is willing to let scripts draw on. A port the domain
-- owns and an adapter implements, in the shape every other host service
-- already has.
--
-- It speaks gobs and events and nothing else. That is what lets one gob tree
-- reach a desktop window, a phone and a browser without the tree knowing
-- which: the toolkit lives entirely on the far side, and swapping it is
-- swapping an adapter.
entity ScreenPort {
    -- Whether there is a display behind this port at all. A machine with no
    -- screen still answers questions about one; see the metric rules.
    has_a_display: boolean

    root_gob: values.GobStorage?     -- what INIT-TOP-WINDOW was handed
    windows: ScreenWindow with port = this

    -- The last thing this port was asked to paint, which is the same list
    -- every other port would have been handed for the same tree.
    last_painted: PaintList?

    -- Which renderer sits behind it. The kinds differ in how they execute a
    -- paint list and in nothing else a script can see, which is the whole
    -- claim this spec makes.
    renderer: DesktopScreen | BrowserScreen

    open_windows: windows where standing = showing
}

-- A window on the operator's own machine, painted by a widget toolkit.
variant DesktopScreen : ScreenPort {
    toolkit: String          -- which one, for a person reading a log
}

-- A browser somewhere else, painted by whatever the page runs.
--
-- The path that existed before this read a layout block straight into markup
-- and never made a gob at all. It worked, and it was a second implementation
-- of the dialect rather than a third renderer of one: the two would drift,
-- and only one of them was what REBOL's own library talks to.
--
-- So a browser implements the same port the desktop does. VIEW, UNVIEW,
-- DO-EVENTS and the handler list are the same borrowed REBOL either way, and
-- the only difference is which adapter executes the paint list.
variant BrowserScreen : ScreenPort {
    connected: boolean       -- whether a browser is attached right now
    reported_size: Integer   -- what the browser said its viewport measures
}

-- One window on the operator's screen, backing one gob.
entity ScreenWindow {
    port: ScreenPort
    gob: values.GobStorage
    standing: WindowStanding

    transitions standing {
        asked_for -> showing
        showing -> dismissed
        terminal: dismissed
    }
}

-- Something that happened on the screen, waiting for the script to be told.
--
-- It is an entity rather than a value because it has a lifetime: the toolkit
-- makes it on its own thread and the interpreter takes it on another, and
-- the gap between those two moments is the whole reason this queue exists.
entity ScreenEvent {
    port: ScreenPort
    kind: ScreenEventKind
    window: ScreenWindow
    delivered: boolean
    across: Integer?            -- present when the event has a position or a size
    down: Integer?
    key: String?                -- a character, or one of system/catalog's key names
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Taking the root gob
------------------------------------------------------------

-- INIT-TOP-WINDOW is handed `system/view/screen-gob` and makes it the root
-- everything else hangs under. It does three things and the C does them in
-- three lines: it remembers the gob, it clears its parent, and it writes the
-- screen's size onto it.
--
-- Writing the size matters more than it looks. VIEW centres a window with
-- `screen/size - window/size / 2`, so a root of the wrong size puts every
-- centred window in the wrong place, and a root of no size puts them all at
-- the same place.
rule TheRootGobIsRememberedAndTakesTheScreensSize {
    when: script_initialises_the_top_window(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display

    ensures:
        run.interpreter.screen_port.root_gob = gob
        gob.parent = absent
        gob.size = the_size_of_display(run.interpreter.screen_port, 0)
}

-- A machine with no screen accepts the root gob and sizes it at nothing.
--
-- This is the decision in this spec most worth defending, because refusing
-- would have been the obvious reading. `view-funcs.reb` calls
-- INIT-VIEW-SYSTEM on its own last line, so INIT-TOP-WINDOW runs while the
-- file is still loading. If it raised on a machine with no display, the file
-- would stop partway and define nothing below the line it stopped on -- and
-- a build server has no display, which means the library would be a
-- different library in the one place it is tested.
--
-- Zero is a real answer rather than a stand-in, which is what makes this
-- honest rather than convenient. The posix host implements two metrics and
-- returns 0 for the other ten, so a script that reads `work-size` and gets
-- 0x0 is reading exactly what a GTK build tells it. Asking about a screen
-- that is not there is answerable; putting a window on it is not, and that
-- is the request that refuses.
rule TheRootGobOfAScreenThatIsNotThereIsSizedAtNothing {
    when: script_initialises_the_top_window(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: not run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display

    ensures:
        run.interpreter.screen_port.root_gob = gob
        gob.parent = absent
        gob.size = zero_pair()
}

-- INIT-TOP-WINDOW answers unset, with a screen or without one.
--
-- The Windows host, the only one Rebol's build compiles a view into, breaks
-- out of the command's case and returns RXR_UNSET at the foot of the
-- dispatcher. The posix host's copy has that return commented out and falls
-- off the end of the function, so it has no answer to agree with and the
-- Windows host is the reading taken. Nothing in the library reads the
-- answer: INIT-VIEW-SYSTEM calls the command for its effect and moves on.
rule TakingTheRootGobAnswersUnset {
    when: script_initialises_the_top_window(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)

    ensures: InitTopWindowResult(value: unset_value())
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Asking about the screen
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Eleven of the twelve metrics answer a pair, and both hosts answer them by
-- asking the operating system two questions and putting the answers side by
-- side.
rule AMetricOfTwoNumbersAnswersAPair {
    when: script_asks_for_a_metric(run, metric, display)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: metric != screens

    ensures: MetricResult(value: the_pair_for(run.interpreter.screen_port, metric, display))
}

-- The count of displays is the one metric that is not a measurement, so it
-- answers an integer. The C writes it into the frame as RXT_INTEGER and
-- returns before reaching the code that would have made a pair of it.
rule TheNumberOfDisplaysAnswersAnInteger {
    when: script_asks_for_a_metric(run, metric, display)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: metric = screens

    ensures: MetricResult(value: the_count_of_displays(run.interpreter.screen_port))
}

-- A title bar has a height and no width, so the pair's first number is
-- always zero. Both hosts set `x = 0` and ask the system only for the
-- height, and VIEW then uses the whole pair as a window's offset --
-- `window/offset: system/view/metrics/title-size` -- which is what makes the
-- zero load-bearing rather than decorative. A width there would push every
-- window sideways.
rule TheTitleBarHasNoWidth {
    when: script_asks_for_a_metric(run, metric, display)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: metric = title_size

    ensures: MetricResult(value: a_pair_whose_first_number_is_zero())
}

-- A word that is not one of the twelve is refused rather than answered.
--
-- Refused rather than answered with none, because the C returns
-- RXR_BAD_ARGS, and because a metric is a number a caller is about to
-- compute with. A none flowing into `screen/size - window/size / 2` fails
-- somewhere else entirely, and the script's author reads a subtraction
-- error rather than the name they got wrong.
--
-- `virtual-screen-size` is the case that shows this is not hypothetical: it
-- is in the word list boot/window.reb hands the host, so it looks supported,
-- and neither host has a branch for it.
rule AMetricNameNoHostServesIsRefused {
    when: script_asks_for_a_metric(run, metric, display)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: not the_metric_is_one_of_the_twelve(metric)

    ensures: run.conclusion = raised
}

-- A display index outside the range of displays that exist is refused.
--
-- A departure from the C, and stated as one. Both hosts read the index and
-- the posix one then ignores it entirely, so asking about display seven on a
-- laptop answers the laptop's numbers. That is silently wrong in the way this
-- project keeps finding: the caller gets a plausible number, computes a
-- window position from it, and the window appears on the wrong screen with
-- nothing anywhere saying why.
--
-- Counting from zero, so a machine with one display serves index 0 and
-- nothing else, and a negative index is refused like any other.
rule ADisplayThatIsNotThereIsRefused {
    when: script_asks_for_a_metric(run, metric, display)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display
    requires: display < 0 or display >= the_count_of_displays(run.interpreter.screen_port)

    ensures: run.conclusion = raised
}

-- Every metric of a screen that is not there is zero. See
-- TheRootGobOfAScreenThatIsNotThereIsSizedAtNothing for why this answers
-- rather than refusing.
rule EveryMetricOfAScreenThatIsNotThereIsZero {
    when: script_asks_for_a_metric(run, metric, display)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: not run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display
    requires: the_metric_is_one_of_the_twelve(metric)

    ensures: MetricResult(value: zero_for(metric))
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Showing
------------------------------------------------------------

-- SHOW makes the operating system's windows match the gob tree. It is one
-- verb doing three jobs -- opening, refreshing and closing -- and which job
-- it does is read off the gob rather than said by the caller.
--
-- That is why UNVIEW can be four lines. It removes the window's gob from the
-- screen's pane and calls SHOW on it, and the removal is what turns the same
-- call from "refresh this" into "close this". Nothing anywhere says "close".

-- Given the root gob, reconcile everything: close every window whose gob has
-- left the pane, then open every gob in the pane that has not got one yet.
--
-- Closing before opening, which is the order the C walks and is not
-- arbitrary. A host with a fixed number of window slots that opened first
-- could run out while holding slots for windows already dismissed.
rule ShowingTheRootReconcilesEveryWindow {
    when: script_shows(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display
    requires: gob = run.interpreter.screen_port.root_gob

    ensures:
        for window in run.interpreter.screen_port.windows where standing = dismissed:
            window_is_closed(window)
        for child in gob.pane where has_no_window(child):
            ScreenWindow.created(
                port: run.interpreter.screen_port,
                gob: child,
                standing: showing)
}

-- Given a gob in the root's pane with no window yet, open one for it.
rule ShowingAGobAskedForOpensItsWindow {
    when: script_shows(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display
    requires: the_window_for(gob).standing = asked_for

    ensures: the_window_for(gob).standing = showing
}

-- Given a gob whose window is already open, repaint it where it stands.
rule ShowingAGobAlreadyOpenRefreshesIt {
    when: script_shows(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display
    requires: the_window_for(gob).standing = showing

    ensures: the_window_is_repainted(the_window_for(gob))
}

-- Given a gob somewhere inside a window -- a button, a slider's knob, any face
-- -- repaint the window that holds it. The window is found by walking up the
-- gob's parents until one is in the root's pane.
--
-- This is the SHOW a GUI makes most: every face that changes shows its own
-- gob, not its window. The C ends OS_Show_Gob with `OS_Draw_Window(0, gob)`,
-- where the 0 means "the window parent of gob". JEBOL ignored such a show
-- until 2026-10-09, so a dragged slider changed its value and the screen never
-- moved.
--
-- A gob that is in no window, or a window that has no window open yet, is
-- shown by nothing, and showing it opens or closes nothing.
rule ShowingAGobInsideAWindowRepaintsThatWindow {
    when: script_shows(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display
    requires: the_window_holding(gob).standing = showing

    ensures: the_window_is_repainted(the_window_holding(gob))
}

-- Given a gob that has a window and has left the root's pane, close it.
rule ShowingAGobThatLeftTheScreenClosesItsWindow {
    when: script_shows(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display
    requires: the_window_for(gob).standing = dismissed

    ensures: window_is_closed(the_window_for(gob))
}

-- SHOW answers whatever it was given, whether or not it did anything.
--
-- The C returns RXR_VALUE without touching the argument slot, so the gob
-- comes straight back out. It matters because VIEW ends `show window` and
-- then `window`, and a caller reading either gets the same thing.
--
-- The declaration accepts none and a block as well as a gob, and the C has a
-- branch for the gob alone. So showing none does nothing and answers none,
-- and that is not an oversight to correct: UNVIEW calls `show window` where
-- window may be none, under a comment saying "none ok".
rule ShowingAnswersWhatItWasGiven {
    when: script_shows(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)

    ensures: ShowResult(value: gob)
}

-- A window JEBOL opens on the desktop carries JEBOL's own icon and name, in
-- its title bar and in the operating system's dock or task bar. Without them
-- the operator sees the Java runtime's mascot and the word "java", which says
-- what the program is built on rather than what it is. The icon is drawn by
-- JEBOL itself rather than read from a file, because the shipped jar carries
-- no pictures. A screen with no display opens no window and shows no icon.
rule ADesktopWindowCarriesJebolsIconAndName {
    when: ScreenWindow.created(port, gob, standing)
    requires: port.has_a_display

    ensures:
        the_window_icon_is(jebols_icon)
        the_application_is_named(jebol)
}

-- Showing needs a screen, and a granted service with nothing behind it is
-- not_present rather than not_granted. The two are different facts about the
-- host and a script may act on either: the first can be fixed by granting
-- and the second cannot.
--
-- This is the one command of the three that refuses without a display,
-- because it is the one that needs one. Asking how big a screen is has an
-- answer when there is no screen; putting a window on it does not.
rule ShowingNeedsADisplay {
    when: script_shows(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: not run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display
    requires: gob_is_a_gob(gob)

    ensures:
        run.conclusion = raised
        run.refusal_reason = not_present
        run.refused_service = windows
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Events
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The screen's own thread never enters the interpreter. It puts an event on
-- a queue and returns, and the interpreter's thread takes it later.
--
-- This is forced rather than chosen. embed.allium already says an
-- interpreter is owned by one thread and that this is what lets series share
-- mutable storage with no synchronisation at all. A toolkit calls a listener
-- on its own thread, so a handler block run from there would be a second
-- thread inside the interpreter, and the damage would be silent: two threads
-- appending to one block corrupt it without either of them failing.
rule AnEventFromTheScreenIsQueuedRatherThanDelivered {
    when: the_screen_reports(port, kind, window)

    ensures: ScreenEvent.created(
        port: port,
        kind: kind,
        window: window,
        delivered: false)
}

-- An event carries what a script needs to act on it, as R3's host fills it.
-- A pointer event -- down, up, move -- carries where the pointer is, counted
-- from the top left of the window's drawable area. A resize carries the
-- drawable area's new size, and a move of the window carries where the
-- window now is on the screen. All three arrive as `event/offset`.
--
-- A key event carries the key, and which kind of event it is depends on the
-- key, as R3's host decides it. A key that types a character is a `key` or
-- `key-up` event and `event/key` is that character. A key that types none is
-- a `control` or `control-up` event and `event/key` is its name from
-- `system/catalog`'s key list -- up, down, left, right, home, end, page-up,
-- page-down, insert, delete, escape, backspace, f1 to f12 and the rest. The C
-- reads a control event's key as a position in that list and a key event's as
-- a character, so the two cannot be mixed. A key the list does not name is
-- not reported.
--
-- A close carries neither.
rule AScreenEventCarriesItsPositionOrItsKey {
    when: the_screen_reports(port, kind, window)

    ensures:
        if kind = down or kind = up or kind = move:
            the_event_offset_is(the_pointer_position_within(window))
        else if kind = resize:
            the_event_offset_is(the_drawable_size_of(window))
        else if kind = offset:
            the_event_offset_is(the_screen_position_of(window))
        else if kind = key or kind = key_up or kind = control or kind = control_up:
            the_event_key_is(the_key_pressed)
}

-- A browser keeps the rule above the same way a desktop window does. The page
-- posts each pointer event -- down, up and move, move included -- with where
-- the pointer is on the page, in whole pixels; JEBOL takes the window's own
-- place off, so the event counts from the window's top left as a desktop
-- window's does. The page posts each key with what it typed, as the number of
-- the character, or, for a key that types nothing, with its name from the
-- catalogue's key list.
--
-- A number rather than the character itself, because the page's message is
-- read as flat fields and a comma typed as a key would split it.
--
-- What cannot be delivered whole is not delivered at all: a pointer event with
-- no position, or one whose position is not two whole numbers; a key event
-- whose number is no character; a control event whose name the catalogue does
-- not know. Delivering any of them would hand the GUI an event R3's host never
-- makes -- a click with no offset fails `event/offset:` in the 2010 GUI as it
-- does in R3. Until 2026-10-09 the page posted only the kind, so every click in
-- a browser was such an event and no GUI could be used there.
rule ABrowsersEventCarriesWhatADesktopWindowsDoes {
    when: the_browser_posts(port, kind, fields)

    ensures:
        if can_be_delivered_whole(kind, fields):
            the_screen_reports(port, kind, the_first_window_showing(port))
        else:
            nothing_is_queued
}

-- Reading `event/key` of a key event whose stored number is no character --
-- which `make event! [type: 'key key: 'f12]` leaves, because the C stores a
-- named key in the top half of the number and reads a key event's number as a
-- character -- raises out-of-range. A real 3.22.5 answers a character past
-- the end of Unicode there, which JEBOL cannot hold. Raising is the nearest
-- answer that is still a REBOL error rather than a host failure.
rule ReadingAKeyThatIsNoCharacterIsRefused {
    when: EvaluatePath(event, key)
    requires: event.type_index = key_event or event.type_index = key_up_event
    requires: not is_a_character(event.data)

    ensures: PathFails(error: out_of_range)
}

-- Waiting on the event port is where queued events become handler calls, and
-- it happens on the thread that started the run. So a block written in a
-- handler sees the same interpreter every other block sees.
rule WaitingOnTheEventPortDeliversWhatIsQueued {
    when: script_waits_on_the_event_port(run)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)

    ensures:
        for event in undelivered_events_of(run.interpreter.screen_port):
            the_handlers_are_run_for(event)
            event.delivered = true
}

-- The same holds when the event port is one of several things waited on, as
-- in `wait [port 0]` or `wait [port 0:00:01]`. Everything that arrives before
-- the time is up is handed to the handlers, and the wait returns the port if a
-- handler woke it, or none when the time runs out. The C does the same: a
-- WAIT on a block runs every port's awake through the system port, the event
-- port among them.
--
-- JEBOL ignored the screen in this form until 2026-10-09, and that hid a crash
-- from the sliders example's end-to-end test: it waited on `[port 0]`, so no
-- event the operator made ever reached a handler, and every check that the
-- example "survived" passed because nothing ran.
rule WaitingOnTheEventPortAmongOthersDeliversWhatArrivesInTime {
    when: script_waits_on_the_event_port_with_a_timeout(run, timeout)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)

    ensures:
        for event in events_arriving_before(run.interpreter.screen_port, timeout):
            the_handlers_are_run_for(event)
            event.delivered = true
        the_wait_returns(run)
}

-- The wait ends when the last window has closed, and not before.
--
-- REBOL's own words: `ep/awake` in `init-view-system` ends with `tail?
-- system/view/screen-gob`, and that is true exactly when the screen has no
-- children left. So VIEW returns when the operator closes the window, which
-- is what makes a script ending in `view [...]` a program rather than a
-- statement.
rule TheWaitEndsWhenTheScreenHasNoWindowsLeft {
    when: script_waits_on_the_event_port(run)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: run.interpreter.screen_port.open_windows.count = 0

    ensures: the_wait_returns(run)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- The paint list
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a gob tree becomes before anything paints it.
--
-- Three renderers are wanted -- a desktop window, a phone and a browser --
-- and the question that decides whether they stay alike is where the tree
-- gets walked. If each renderer walks it, each has its own opinion about
-- offsets, clipping and opacity, and the three drift apart in ways only a
-- person comparing screenshots would notice.
--
-- So the tree is walked once, here, into a flat list of instructions that
-- carry absolute positions. A renderer executes the list and decides nothing.
-- The three cannot disagree about where a thing goes, because none of them
-- works it out.
--
-- It is also what makes the DRAW dialect tractable later: thirty commands
-- become thirty instruction kinds added once rather than three times.
enum PaintKind {
    fill          -- a rectangle of one colour
    | writing     -- a line of characters
    | picture     -- an image, pixel for pixel
}

-- One thing to paint, with everywhere it needs to go already worked out.
--
-- Absolute rather than relative, and its own clip rather than a stack. Both
-- for the same reason: an instruction that means the same thing on its own,
-- out of order and with no state carried between them, is one a renderer
-- cannot execute differently from its neighbour. A clip stack would need
-- push and pop to survive the wire and would let a renderer lose its place.
value PaintInstruction {
    kind: PaintKind
    across: Integer          -- from the left of the whole surface
    down: Integer            -- from the top of it
    wide: Integer
    high: Integer
    clip: ClipRectangle      -- absolute, already intersected with every parent
    opacity: Integer         -- 0 to 255, already multiplied down the tree
}

value ClipRectangle {
    across: Integer
    down: Integer
    wide: Integer
    high: Integer
}

-- One gob tree, walked once, as the thing every renderer is handed.
--
-- It exists as a thing with a name rather than as a step inside SHOW because
-- it is the seam the three renderers meet at. A test can build one and assert
-- on it without a screen of any kind, which is how the desktop renderer and
-- the browser are held to the same picture without either of them running.
entity PaintList {
    for_gob: values.GobStorage
    instructions: List<PaintInstruction>

    count: instructions.count
}

-- === How closely two renderers have to agree ================

-- The paint list has to match exactly. The pictures do not, and cannot.
--
-- Two things are being checked and they are not the same check. One renderer
-- and another are handed an identical list of instructions -- that is exact,
-- it is decided here rather than three times, and nothing excuses a
-- difference in it. What each of them then rasterises is another matter:
-- Java2D and a browser's engine both anti-alias, and they disagree about how
-- much of an edge pixel a sloped line covers. No amount of care in either
-- renderer removes that, because it is two different rasterisers and not a
-- fault in the port.
--
-- Which is why the picture comparison held to zero differing pixels for as
-- long as every shape in it was an axis-aligned box. A box has no
-- part-covered edge, so both engines answer the same thing. The first curve,
-- sloped stroke, gradient or letter ends that, and a gate that demanded
-- exactness would have to be either loosened in a hurry or worked around by
-- never drawing a curve in it.
--
-- So the comparison allows a difference where a difference is physical and
-- nowhere else. A pixel in the flat inside of a shape must match: if the two
-- disagree there, one of them executed the instruction differently and that
-- is a defect. A pixel on an edge may differ, because that is where coverage
-- is a judgement.
rule TheInsideOfAShapeMatchesExactly {
    when: two_renderers_execute(the_same_paint_list)
    requires: the_pixel_is_not_on_an_edge_in_either_picture

    ensures: the_two_pixels_are_the_same_colour
}

rule AnEdgePixelMayDifferByTheAllowance {
    when: two_renderers_execute(the_same_paint_list)
    requires: the_pixel_is_on_an_edge_in_either_picture

    ensures: every_channel_differs_by_at_most(the_channel_allowance)
}

-- "On an edge" is decided from the picture rather than from the instructions,
-- because it is the rasteriser's edges that matter and not the geometry's: a
-- pixel is on an edge when the eight around it are not all one colour.
--
-- The two numbers are written here so that widening one is a change to the
-- specification rather than a number somebody nudged in a test. The channel
-- allowance is 24 of 255, which is about a tenth of the range and comfortably
-- more than two engines' coverage arithmetic will differ by on a single
-- sample; and at most a fortieth of the picture may be edge pixels that use
-- any of that allowance, which is far more edge than a page of shapes has and
-- far less than a renderer painting the wrong thing would produce.
config {
    channel_allowance: Integer = 24
    most_edge_pixels_that_may_differ: Decimal = 0.025
}

-- A gob paints itself before its children, and its children in pane order.
--
-- The order is the whole of what "in front" means, and the C's compositor
-- relies on it too. A list in the wrong order is a picture with the wrong
-- thing on top, which reads as a rendering bug in whichever renderer the
-- person happened to be looking at.
rule AGobIsPaintedBeforeItsChildren {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        for gob in every_gob_under(root):
            the_instruction_for(gob) comes before every_instruction_for(gob.pane)
}

-- Every position in the list is measured from the surface, not from a parent.
--
-- A renderer that had to add up offsets would be doing the one piece of
-- arithmetic the three must agree on. Doing it once means a renderer that
-- gets it wrong cannot exist.
rule EveryPositionIsAbsolute {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        for instruction in the_list:
            instruction.across = sum_of_offsets_from(root)
}

-- Every instruction carries the clip it is subject to, already narrowed by
-- every parent between it and the root.
rule EveryInstructionCarriesItsOwnClip {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        for instruction in the_list:
            instruction.clip = the_overlap_of_every_parents_area(instruction)
}

-- A gob with no width or no height contributes nothing, and neither does
-- anything under it.
--
-- Its children would be clipped to nothing anyway, so walking them would
-- produce instructions that paint nothing and cost every renderer the work
-- of discovering that separately.
rule AGobOfNoSizeContributesNothing {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    requires: some_gob_has_no_area(root)
    ensures:
        for gob in every_gob_with_no_area(root):
            no_instruction_exists_for(gob)
            no_instruction_exists_for(gob.pane)
}

-- Opacity multiplies down the tree, so a half-transparent gob inside another
-- half-transparent one is a quarter.
--
-- Multiplied here rather than by each renderer, because compositing is where
-- toolkits differ most and the arithmetic is the part that must not.
rule OpacityMultipliesDownTheTree {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        for instruction in the_list:
            instruction.opacity = every_ancestors_alpha_multiplied_together
}

-- A content kind nothing can paint yet produces no instruction at all.
--
-- Four of the eight paint: a colour, a string, a rich-text block and an
-- image. A draw block, an effect, a native widget and nothing itself produce
-- nothing, and that gap is the same gap in all three renderers because it is
-- decided here.
rule AContentKindWithNothingToPaintProducesNoInstruction {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        for gob in every_gob_under(root) where content_is_not_paintable(gob):
            no_instruction_exists_for(gob)
}

-- A gob whose text is a block holds R3's rich-text dialect, and only its
-- strings are written. `font` followed by an object sets the size from its
-- `size`, the colour from its `color`, bold or italic from its `style` and
-- the shadow from its `shadow`; `para` followed by an object lays the line out
-- (see AGobsTextIsLaidOutByItsPara); `anti-alias` followed by its switch is
-- read past. The block's own source is never written: the 2010 R3 GUI hands
-- every label `[font make object! [...] para make object! [...] anti-alias on
-- "These will change:"]`, and forming that whole wrote the font's fields
-- across the window.
rule AGobsRichTextWritesOnlyItsStrings {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        for gob in every_gob_under(root) where its_text_is_a_block(gob):
            the_instructions_for(gob) write only the_strings_of(gob.text)
}

-- A gob's text is laid out inside the gob by its para. The para's `origin`
-- moves the space the text has in from the top left, and its `margin` takes
-- the same from the bottom right. Within what is left, `align` puts the line
-- at the left, the centre or the right, and `valign` puts it at the top, the
-- middle or the bottom. A para that says nothing about a field leaves it as
-- `system/standard/para` has it: origin 2x2, margin 2x2, left, top. A gob
-- whose text is a plain string has that standard para.
--
-- The line is placed as one piece, all its runs together, so a centred line
-- of a plain word and a bold word is centred as a whole. Where each run
-- starts follows from how wide the runs before it really are, and that is
-- measured by whatever writes the text, because only it knows the font.
--
-- The C's layout is in the rich-text engine, which is not in the vendored
-- source; `host-text.c` shows what it is handed -- origin and margin as
-- pairs, align left, center or right, valign top, middle or bottom -- and the
-- defaults are `view-funcs.reb`'s. The 2010 GUI's buttons are `align:
-- 'center valign: 'middle` and its radio buttons `origin: 18x0`, which is
-- where the label starts so as to clear the circle. JEBOL ignored the para
-- until 2026-10-09: every button label sat at its top left and every radio
-- label was written over its circle.
rule AGobsTextIsLaidOutByItsPara {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        for gob in every_gob_under(root) where it_has_text(gob):
            the_line_is_placed(gob,
                the_box_of(gob, the_para_of(gob).origin, the_para_of(gob).margin),
                the_para_of(gob).align,
                the_para_of(gob).valign)
}

-- A font with a `shadow` pair writes its text twice: first in black, moved by
-- the pair, then in its own colour where it belongs. `host-text.c` reads the
-- shadow as an offset, a colour and a blur; `view-funcs.reb`'s standard font
-- has none. The 2010 GUI's buttons have `shadow: 2x2`, which is what makes
-- their white labels stand out from the blue.
rule AFontWithAShadowWritesItTwice {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        for gob in every_gob_under(root) where has_a_shadow(the_font_of(gob)):
            the_line_is_written_first(gob, black, the_font_of(gob).shadow)
}

-- A gob's text runs over as many lines as it has newlines plus one. Each line
-- is as tall as the tallest run on it -- its ascent and descent -- and the
-- lines are stacked from the top of the space the para leaves; a line is
-- placed across by the para's align, and the whole stack down by its valign.
-- A newline is where a line ends and is not itself written. The comment area
-- of a form is the case that needs it: it holds what a person typed, Enter
-- and all.
rule AGobsTextBreaksAtItsNewlines {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        for gob in every_gob_under(root) where it_has_text(gob):
            the_lines_written(gob) = the_text_split_at_newlines(gob)
}

-- A para whose `wrap?` is true also breaks a line that is too wide for the
-- room its origin and margin leave across. The break comes before the word
-- that would not fit, and the spaces after the last word that did stay on
-- its line, hanging past the edge rather than starting the next one. A word
-- too wide for the room on a line of its own is broken where it overflows,
-- with at least one character on each line. A para that says nothing about
-- `wrap?` takes it from `system/standard/para`, which has it true, and so
-- does a plain string. A wrapped line is a line like any other: it is placed by
-- align, counted by SIZE-TEXT, and a caret moves over it the same way. The
-- 2010 GUI's comment area is `wrap?: true` and its fields `wrap?: false`.
-- The C's wrapping is in the rich-text engine, which is not in the vendored
-- source, so the breaking rule here is the ordinary one for a word processor.
rule AParaThatWrapsBreaksALineTooWideForItsRoom {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        for gob in every_gob_under(root) where the_para_of(gob).wrap and it_has_text(gob):
            every_line_fits(gob, the_box_of(gob, the_para_of(gob).origin, the_para_of(gob).margin))
                or the_line_is_one_overflowing_character(gob)
}

-- How wide and how tall text is, is the screen's to say: it is the screen
-- that has the font. A desktop window and a page answer from the same
-- measurement, so a caret worked out for one sits where the other draws the
-- text. Measuring is part of drawing, so a screen with no display refuses it
-- as it refuses SHOW: not present.
rule TextIsMeasuredByTheScreen {
    when: the_script_measures_text(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: not run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display

    ensures: MeasuringFails(error: not_present)
}

-- SIZE-TEXT answers how much room the gob's text takes: the widest line
-- across, and every line's height added together down. The para's origin and
-- margin are the gob's to add -- the 2010 R3 GUI adds them itself -- so they
-- are not in the answer. A gob with no text, or text with no string in it, is
-- 0x0; a string with no characters is still a line, one line tall.
rule SizeTextIsTheRoomTheTextTakes {
    when: the_script_asks(size_text, gob)
    requires: run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display

    ensures: the_answer_is(the_widest_line(gob) by the_lines_heights_added(gob))
}

-- CARET-TO-OFFSET answers where a caret before a character is drawn, from the
-- gob's own top left: across, the line's start as the para places it plus
-- the width of the text before the caret on that line; down, the top of the
-- line. The string is named by its place in the rich-text block -- an integer,
-- or the block at that place -- and the character by its place in the string
-- -- an integer, or the string at that place, which is how the 2010 GUI asks.
-- A place past the end is the end.
rule CaretToOffsetIsWhereTheCaretIsDrawn {
    when: the_script_asks(caret_to_offset, gob, element, position)
    requires: run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display

    ensures: the_answer_is(the_place_a_caret_is_drawn(gob, element, position))
}

-- OFFSET-TO-CARET is the other way round: given a point in the gob, the
-- caret nearest it. The line is the one the point is level with, the first
-- or last when it is above or below them all; across, the caret is at the
-- character boundary nearest the point, the start or end of the line when it
-- is outside it. The answer is the C's: a copy of the rich-text block,
-- positioned at the string, with that string positioned at the caret -- so
-- `first` of it is the string at the caret, the very string the gob holds, and
-- the 2010 GUI edits it in place. A gob whose text holds no string answers
-- none.
rule OffsetToCaretFindsTheNearestCaret {
    when: the_script_asks(offset_to_caret, gob, point)
    requires: run.interpreter.screen_port.has_a_display

    ensures: the_answer_is(the_block_at_the_caret_nearest(gob, point))
}

-- Rich text may hold `caret` followed by an object with three fields, caret,
-- start and end, each a block of two: where in the rich-text block, and where
-- in that string. The caret is drawn as a line one pixel wide, the height of
-- its line, before the character it names, in the colour of that run's text;
-- when start and end are both set and differ, the text between them is
-- marked by a fill behind it. Neither is drawn while caret is unset. The
-- object is the caret's and not a font, so nothing in it changes the text.
--
-- R3's text engine draws both from that object; the 2010 GUI puts it in
-- every editable field's text. JEBOL read it as a font until 2026-10-09, and
-- drew no caret, so a field being typed into showed no place to type.
rule ARichTextCaretIsDrawn {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        for gob in every_gob_under(root) where its_caret_is_set(gob):
            a_caret_is_drawn_at(the_place_a_caret_is_drawn(gob, its_caret_element(gob), its_caret_position(gob)))
}

-- A window's text is its title, and the screen's text is a label. Neither is
-- painted.
--
-- The same field means two things depending on where the gob sits, which is
-- why this needs saying. On an ordinary gob {@code text} is content and the
-- C paints it: GOBT_STRING. On a window it is what goes in the title bar --
-- VIEW writes {@code window/text: any [opts/title window/text "REBOL:
-- untitled"]} and the host reads it when it opens the window. And the screen
-- gob carries {@code text: "Top Gob"}, which is a name for the thing rather
-- than anything anybody should see.
--
-- Painting them is the failure this rule exists to stop, and it is a quiet
-- one: a window shows its own title written across its top left corner, in
-- black, over whatever was meant to be there. It was in the desktop renderer
-- and nobody noticed, because no test gob had any text. The browser found it
-- the first time a real VIEW ran through it, which is what sharing one paint
-- list is for.
rule TheScreensTextAndAWindowsTitleAreNotPainted {
    when: FlattenTheTree(root)
    ensures:
        no_instruction_exists_for(the_text_of(root))
        for window in root.pane:
            no_instruction_exists_for(the_text_of(window))
}

-- Every renderer executes the same list, and that is the whole mechanism.
--
-- Not a promise to be checked afterwards by comparing pictures. The list is
-- the single input, so a difference between two renderers can only be a
-- difference in how one of them draws a stated rectangle at a stated place --
-- never a difference of opinion about where the rectangle is.
--
-- What comparing pictures is still for: glyph shapes and anti-aliased edges,
-- which no two rasterisers agree on and which no amount of shared input
-- fixes. Those want a tolerance. Geometry and colour want none.
rule EveryRendererExecutesTheSameList {
    when: script_shows(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)

    ensures:
        for renderer in every_renderer_attached(run):
            renderer_is_given(the_list_flattened_from(gob))
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- The browser as a screen
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Nothing about how the list reaches a browser belongs in the port.
--
-- The port hands over a paint list and takes events back. Whether that
-- travels as server-sent events, as a socket, or through a host's own web
-- framework is the host's business, and JEBOL exists to run inside a host
-- that already has one. A transport in the domain would be a decision made
-- for every host by a language.
rule TheTransportIsNotThePortsBusiness {
    when: script_shows(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: the_screen_is_a_browser(run)
    ensures: the_paint_list_is_offered_to_whatever_is_attached(run)
}

-- A browser that is not attached is a screen that is not there.
--
-- The same split every screen makes: measurements answer zero and taking the
-- root gob is accepted, so the library still loads, and showing refuses. A
-- host that started a browser screen and has nobody looking at it is in
-- exactly the position of a machine with no display.
rule ABrowserWithNobodyAttachedIsAScreenThatIsNotThere {
    when: script_shows(run, gob)
    requires: windows_are_granted(run)
    requires: the_screen_is_a_browser(run)

    requires: not the_browser_is_attached(run)

    ensures:
        run.conclusion = raised
        run.refusal_reason = not_present
        run.refused_service = windows
}

-- An event from a browser names a gob and joins the same queue every other
-- event joins.
--
-- Which is what lets one handler block work on a desktop and in a browser
-- without knowing which it is running under. The round trip is real and the
-- script cannot see it: an event arrives, the interpreter's own thread takes
-- it inside WAIT, and the handlers run exactly where they always run.
rule AnEventFromABrowserJoinsTheSameQueue {
    when: the_browser_reports(port, kind, window)

    ensures: ScreenEvent.created(
        port: port,
        kind: kind,
        window: window,
        delivered: false)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A window on the screen is a window the root gob knows about. The two can
-- disagree for as long as it takes SHOW to run and no longer, which is what
-- makes SHOW a reconciliation rather than a command.
invariant EveryOpenWindowHangsUnderTheRootGob {
    for port in ScreenPort:
        for window in port.open_windows:
            window.gob.parent = port.root_gob
}

-- Nothing the screen reports is acted on until the interpreter's own thread
-- takes it. An event created by the toolkit and already delivered would mean
-- a handler had run somewhere else.
invariant AnEventIsOnlyDeliveredByTheInterpretersOwnThread {
    for event in ScreenEvent:
        event.delivered implies event_was_taken_inside_a_wait(event)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surface
------------------------------------------------------------

surface TheScreen {
    facing host: ScreenPort

    provides:
        InitialiseTheTopWindow(host, gob)
        AskForAMetric(host, metric, display)
        Show(host, gob)
        TakeQueuedEvents(host)

    exposes:
        ScreenMetric
        WindowStanding
        ScreenEventKind

    @guarantee TheToolkitLivesEntirelyBehindThisPort
        -- Four requests cross this boundary and every one of them is about
        -- gobs, metrics and events. Nothing on this side names a widget
        -- toolkit, a drawing surface or a window handle.
        --
        -- That is what makes the three targets one design rather than three
        -- programs. A desktop window, a phone and a browser differ in how
        -- they paint a gob and in nothing else a script can see, so the same
        -- description reaches all three and the difference is which adapter
        -- is supplied.
        --
        -- The alternative was tried and is still in the tree: the existing
        -- web renderer reads a layout block straight into markup and never
        -- makes a gob at all. It works, and it is a second implementation of
        -- the dialect rather than a third renderer of one, so the two will
        -- drift and only one of them will be the one that REBOL's own
        -- library talks to.

    @guarantee AScreenThatIsNotThereAnswersAboutItselfAndRefusesToDrawOnItself
        -- The three commands split on whether they need pixels. Taking the
        -- root gob and answering a metric do not, and they answer zeros.
        -- Showing does, and it refuses with not_present.
        --
        -- The split is what lets the library load on a machine with no
        -- display, which every build server is and which is therefore the
        -- only machine the library is ever tested on. A port that refused
        -- all three would make `view-funcs.reb` stop partway there and run
        -- whole everywhere else, so the tested library and the shipped
        -- library would be different libraries.

    @guarantee TheScreensThreadNeverEntersTheInterpreter
        -- Events are queued by the toolkit and taken by the interpreter's
        -- own thread inside WAIT. A handler block therefore runs on the
        -- thread that started the run, sees the same bindings every other
        -- block sees, and is bounded by the same deadline.
        --
        -- One thread per interpreter is what embed.allium relies on to let
        -- series share mutable storage without synchronising. A listener
        -- running a handler on the toolkit's thread would break that
        -- silently: two threads appending to one block leave it wrong
        -- without either of them failing.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "VIEW without /no-wait ends in DO-EVENTS, which is `wait system/ports/event`, and that returns only when the last window has closed. This spec says it blocks, which is what the C does and what a desktop script wants. It sits badly with the rest of JEBOL: a host runs a script under a deadline and a nesting limit precisely so that a request thread cannot be parked, and a granted screen would be the one way past that. FinishedRunsRespectTheirDeadline in embed.allium says a finished run respected its deadline, so either the wait is interruptible by the deadline or that invariant has an exception in it. The reading taken here is that a run with no deadline waits forever and a run with one is ended by it, because that keeps both true and needs no special case. Whether blocking at all is the right default is the part to revisit: it may make a poor experience for a host that expected to keep serving requests."

open question "Should a metric of a screen that is not there be zero, or should the three commands split some other way? Zero is what the posix host answers for the ten metrics it does not implement, so it is a real answer rather than a stand-in, and it is what lets the library load on a build server. The cost is that a script cannot tell 'this display is genuinely at the origin' from 'there is no display'. Nothing in REBOL's own library reads a metric and branches on whether it is zero, so the cost is currently theoretical."

open question "Which gob content kinds paint in this increment? Colour, text and image, because those need no dialect. A gob whose content is a draw block currently paints nothing at all rather than refusing, which is the quiet failure this project keeps finding elsewhere: it will look like a blank window rather than like a missing feature. Should it refuse until the DRAW dialect lands, so that the gap has a name?"

open question "GUI-METRIC declares a /set refinement for the writable keywords -- restore, minimize, maximize and activate are in the word list -- and neither host reads the value it is given. The posix host reads the /display index and nothing else. Are those four words window controls that were never finished, or metrics that were never made writable? Nothing in the mezzanine calls GUI-METRIC/SET, so there is no second reading to check against."

open question "What size is a window, as against the gob inside it? VIEW builds a parent gob at the child's size and then offsets it by title-size, which reads as though the gob's size is the drawable area and the title bar sits outside it. Both hosts also serve border-size and border-fixed, which only matter if something is meant to add them. Nothing in view-funcs.reb adds them, so either the host is expected to, or they are there for a layout engine that was never written."

open question "Which window does an event on a page belong to? A browser paints every window onto one surface, so a click comes back with no way to say which window it was in, and the server hands the first one that is showing to the screen. With one window open that is right and with two it is a coin toss. Three ways out and none has been chosen: the page could carry the flattened windows' rectangles and name the one the click landed in; the paint list could carry a window identifier per instruction so the page can report it; or a page could be declared to hold one window, which is the smallest change and the one that makes VIEW of a second window an error rather than a guess. It wants deciding before anything is built on it, because a handler that fires on the wrong window is a defect nothing in the language can catch."
